:"John O'Connell Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.047899,"lo":-135.340627,"d":573,"ds":"The John O'Connell Bridge is a cable-stayed bridge over the Sitka Channel located in Sitka, Alaska. The bridge connects the town of Sitka on Baranof Island to the airport and Coast Guard Station on Japonski Island. Until the bridge was completed in 1971, the commute was only achievable through a ferry service.","wpu":"John O'Connell Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/John_O%27Connell_Bridge%2C_Sitka_2013.JPG/960px-John_O%27Connell_Bridge%2C_Sitka_2013.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. Peter's by-the-Sea Episcopal Church (Sitka, Alaska)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.05152,"lo":-135.32799,"d":863,"ds":"St. Peter's by-the-Sea Episcopal Church, also known as St. Peter's by the Sea or St.","wpu":"St. Peter's by-the-Sea Episcopal Church (Sitka, Alaska)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/St._Peter%27s-by-the-Sea_Episcopal_Church.jpg/960px-St._Peter%27s-by-the-Sea_Episcopal_Church.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sitka National Cemetery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.054958,"lo":-135.323802,"d":1121,"ds":"Sitka National Cemetery is a United States National Cemetery located in the city of Sitka, Alaska. It encompasses 4.3 acres (1.7 ha), and as of the end of 2005 had 1,049 interments. It is administered as part of the Fort Richardson National Cemetery by the United States Department of Veterans Affairs.","wpu":"Sitka National Cemetery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Sitka13%28js%29.jpg/960px-Sitka13%28js%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mount Verstovia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.059167,"lo":-135.265833,"d":4656,"ds":"Mount Verstovia is a prominent peak located just 2.6 miles east of the center of Sitka, Alaska, on Baranof Island, Alexander Archipelago.","wpu":"Mount Verstovia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Mt._Verstovia.jpg/960px-Mt._Verstovia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fortress of the Bear","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.050556,"lo":-135.233056,"d":6594,"ds":"The Fortress of the Bear is a non-profit bear refuge and education center in Sitka, Alaska. It was established in 2003 by Les and Evy Kinnear at the Gary Paxton Industrial Park, which previously housed the Sitka Pulp Mill. Sitka conservation organizations opposed the Kinnears' plan to open a refuge, believing that wild bears should not be confined in tanks and shown to the public.","wpu":"Fortress of the Bear","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Fortress_of_the_Bear.jpg/960px-Fortress_of_the_Bear.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Old Sitka Site","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.12955,"lo":-135.37342,"d":8721,"ds":"The Redoubt St. Archangel Michael Site, also known as the Old Sitka Site and now in Old Sitka State Historical Park, is a National Historic Landmark near Sitka, Alaska. Now of archaeological interest, the site, about 7 miles (11 km) north of Sitka at the end of Halibut Point Road, was the site of the early Russian-American Company settlement known as Redoubt St.","wpu":"Old Sitka Site","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/SitkaRussianPossessionPlate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"Outer Coast is an unaccredited private college in Sitka, Alaska, United States. It opened a two-year undergraduate program in fall 2024. Outer Coast admitted a freshman class of 20 students in 2024 and a similar number in 2025, for a total student body of about 40.","wpu":"Outer Coast College","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Outer_Coast_Logo.png/960px-Outer_Coast_Logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}